    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors in Our World</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Purple Flower.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Identify a few familiar words from a story by connecting them to prior knowledge. Concept: Use a word and a gesture or drawing to describe a familiar object.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use simple drawings to communicate about objects (e.g., drawing a blue bird). Can use gestures or point to objects to clarify their responses when describing colors. Can describe objects and concepts from a story by connecting prior knowledge with supportive strategies, such as using words and gestures (e.g., saying "bird" while flapping arms).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, using flashcards of previously learned objects and colors (e.g., "blue bird," "red flower," "green leaf"). Teacher asks, "What color is this?" and encourages students to respond with "It's a blue bird." The teacher can also lead a simple "Color Song" if available, pointing to objects of different colors in the classroom. This activates prior knowledge of colors and nouns.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to describe objects using color and a gesture or simple drawing. For example, the teacher holds up a flashcard of a bird, says "This is a bird," then flaps arms and says "A blue bird can fly!" The teacher then draws a simple blue bird on the board while saying "blue bird." The teacher also models the /l/ sound in "blue" and "leaf" by over-emphasizing it.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of colors and actions.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a red flower flashcard and a blue flower flashcard) "Is this a blue flower or a red flower?" (Expected answer: "Red flower.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Flapping arms like a bird) "Do birds fly?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors in Our World."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Picture Story. A simple, large-format picture story or big book with colorful illustrations, focusing on objects found in nature with their colors (e.g., "A Day in the Garden"). The story includes sentences like, "I see a blue bird. It flies in the sky. Look! That's a purple flower. A green leaf grows on the tree. There is an orange cat under the tree." (Genre: Narrative excerpt; Register: Simple, descriptive; Format: Visual storybook).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Recognizing colors and objects: Identify familiar objects by color (e.g., "blue bird" or "red flower."). Describing with colors: Use simple color phrases in sentences (e.g., "That is a red bird.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> While narrating the story, the teacher points to objects and asks simple gist questions or uses TPR.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a blue bird in the story: "What is this?" (Expected: "Bird!" or "Blue bird!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a purple flower: "What color is it?" (Expected: "Purple!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says "Fly!" and students flap their arms.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Color + noun (e.g., "blue bird"), WH-questions ("What color is it?"), Present simple ("It is an orange cat."). Vocabulary: bird, flower, cat, tree, house, leaf, sky, blue, purple, orange, green, red, tall, small, see, look, fly, sit, grow, count, be (is), color, show, that, this, two, three, four, many. Pronunciation: /l/ in "blue" and "leaf," identifying initial sounds (/f/, /b/, /k/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Draw and Show (Controlled Practice)</b>
                        The teacher calls out a familiar object and its color (e.g., "Draw a red flower!"). Students draw the object on individual mini whiteboards or paper. Once finished, they hold up their drawing and say, "That's a red flower!" The teacher provides feedback on their drawing and pronunciation. This activity encourages mediation through drawing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Guess the Gesture (Semi-controlled)</b>
                        The teacher says an object (e.g., "bird," "tree," "cat," "flower"). Students respond by saying the word and making a gesture that describes it (e.g., flapping arms for "bird," standing tall and reaching up for "tree," pretending to pet for "cat," holding hands open for "flower"). The teacher then asks, "What color is it?" and students respond with a color. This promotes mediation through gestures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Describe My Drawing (Pair Interaction/Peer Check)</b>
                        Students work in pairs. Each student draws two or three colored objects (e.g., a "green leaf" and a "blue sky") on their paper. Then, they exchange drawings with their partner. The partner points to an object in the drawing and describes it using color + noun, "That is a green leaf." The drawing acts as a mediated message for the partner to interpret and describe.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors in Our World."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> "Color Nature Walk" Project Explanation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Small Group "Show and Explain" with Drawings and Gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a large sheet of paper and crayons.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reminds them about the "Color Nature Walk" project (observing and collecting colorful objects). "Imagine you went on a color nature walk outside our school. What colors and things did you see?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group discusses and draws 3-4 colorful objects they might have seen (e.g., a blue sky, a green leaf, a purple flower, an orange cat, a red bird, a tall tree). The drawings serve as their "color chart" for the walk.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once drawings are complete, each group takes turns presenting their "Color Nature Walk" chart to the class. One student from the group points to an object in their drawing (e.g., a blue bird). Another student from the group describes it, "This is a blue bird!" A third student can make a gesture to clarify (e.g., flapping arms for the bird).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages all group members to participate in pointing, describing, and gesturing. The drawings and gestures are crucial for mediating their message about their imagined "Color Nature Walk."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe each student's ability to participate, use target vocabulary, and employ drawings/gestures for mediation during the group presentation and individual task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Individual "Show Me a Color" Drawing and Gesture. Each student receives a small piece of paper. The teacher asks them to draw one object from their "Color Nature Walk" that they remember well (e.g., a blue bird, a green leaf, a purple flower). After drawing, each student individually shows their drawing to the teacher, says its name and color (e.g., "It's a blue bird!"), and makes a simple gesture related to the object (e.g., flapping arms for the bird, pointing to the sky for sky).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of colors and object nouns (e.g., "blue," "bird," "flower").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target grammar: Color + noun phrase (e.g., "blue bird," "purple flower").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in using simple drawings and/or gestures to communicate about the object and its color.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort in attempting to mediate their message.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick drawing-based exit ticket.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Draw a happy face if you can draw a blue bird. Draw a sad face if it's hard.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Draw a happy face if you can show with your hands how a bird flies. Draw a sad face if it's hard.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student self-reflection and teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was your favorite color you drew today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was it easy or hard to show your drawings to your friends?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The "Color Nature Walk" project allowed students to integrate previously learned vocabulary and grammar in a meaningful way. The use of drawings and gestures was effective in facilitating mediation for this age group, though some students needed more prompting for gestures. The specific objective of using words and gestures/drawings to describe familiar objects was met by most students. Next time, I could provide more specific sentence frames for the group presentation to further support lower-proficiency students. The connection to the project was strong, making the learning tangible. This concludes the unit; the next lesson will introduce new vocabulary and a new theme, building on the descriptive skills learned here.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson marks the end of the "Colors in Our World" theme. The next lesson will begin a new theme, likely introducing new sets of vocabulary and grammar while reinforcing the communicative strategies developed in this unit, such as describing objects and using gestures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>